<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhimmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhimmi is the status under Islamic law of protected non-Muslim communities, mainly Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, who were permitted to practise their religion in return for paying a poll tax (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>) and accepting certain restrictions. It structured relations between rulers and subject populations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, where non-Muslims long formed the majority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhimmi.jpg" alt="Dhimmi" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mdktb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ALFiqh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dhimmi, 'protected person', refers to the state's obligation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> to protect the life, property and freedom of religion of non-Muslims in return for their loyalty and payment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax. The status was first applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and Sabians, the People of the Book, and was later extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Jains and Buddhists; Muslim governments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> basin readily gave it to the Hindus and Buddhists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-umar">Pact of Umar</a> is traditionally held to be an agreement between the caliph Umar and the Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the seventh century, but academic historians believe its present form was the work of later jurists who attributed it to Umar, and that much of it was borrowed from the Theodosian and Justinian codes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Adult, free, healthy men paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>; women, children, the elderly, slaves, the disabled and travellers were exempt. Dhimmis did not pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a> paid by Muslims but their own religious dues, and they did not bear arms or perform military service. Islamic law forbade the enslavement of free dhimmis. The communities kept their own leaders, courts and personal and religious laws, such as the Jewish Halakhic courts, and a qadi usually could not interfere unless the parties chose Islamic law.</p><p>Restrictions included bans on building new places of worship or repairing old ones without Muslim consent, on loud prayers and church bells, on teaching children the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and on seeking converts among Muslims. Dhimmis were made to wear distinctive dress and to avoid riding on saddles, and in Mamluk Egypt they could not ride even donkeys inside cities. Forced conversion was forbidden, but cases of it are well documented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians judge the system differently. Marshall Hodgson describes an official policy that dhimmis should feel inferior and know their place. Bernard Lewis holds that the restrictions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were social and symbolic rather than practical, and María Rosa Menocal argues that Jews under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> were better off than in Christian Europe.</p><p>In the 19th century pressure from European powers and from Muslim reformers gradually relaxed the inequalities. The Ottoman Reform Edict of 18 February 1856, issued partly under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the United Kingdom, proclaimed equality between Muslims and non-Muslims, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and allowed non-Muslims to join the army. The dhimma and the jizya are no longer imposed in countries with a Muslim majority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhimmi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christians and Jews were protected as <b>dhimmis</b>, paying a poll tax and keeping their own communal leaders and judges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Christians and Jews were tolerated as <b>dhimmis</b> but paid the jizya and the kharaj; about half the population was Muslim by the Norman Conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Conquered non-Muslim populations were treated as protected peoples (<b>dhimmis</b>), who were guaranteed security of life, property and worship in return for paying the jizya and accepting certain legal disabilities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The jizya poll tax on non-Muslim subjects (<b>dhimmis</b>) and the kharaj land tax were the main sources of revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Conversion among the conquered peoples was gradual: Christians, Jews and Zoroastrians were permitted to keep their religions as protected peoples, the <b>dhimmis</b>, paying a special tax, and it took several centuries before Muslims formed the majority in Egypt, Syria, Iraq and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars argue that changing the status of non-Muslims from <b>dhimmi</b>, a subjugated population, to equal subjects clashed with the traditional values of the Muslim population and was in part responsible for the Hamidian massacres and the later Armenian genocide.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
